-- title:
--   Index-two triviality criterion for points of the character closure
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a Noetherian principal ideal domain in which $2$ is irreducible, let $F$ be a field realised as a fraction field of $O$, let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying a cocommutative Hopf algebra structure over $O$ which is finite and free as an $O$-module, and let $L$ be a field which is an algebra over both $F$ and $O$ compatibly, equipped with a valuation $v$ with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero such that $v(r)\le 1$ for every $r$ in the image of $O$. Let $S\le S'$ be finite submonoids of the convolution monoid `WithConv` of $F$-algebra maps $F\otimes_O A\to L$, and assume the index-two condition that $\nu\mu\in S$ whenever $\nu,\mu\in S'\setminus S$. For $T\in\{S,S'\}$ write $\mathrm{ptSet}\,T$ for the set of $F$-algebra maps whose convolution class lies in $T$, and $\mathrm{pointQuot}\,T$ for the quotient of $F\otimes_O A$ by the ideal of elements annihilated by all points of $\mathrm{ptSet}\,T$. Assume for both $T=S$ and $T=S'$: the pairing hypothesis that an element of $\mathrm{pointQuot}\,T\otimes_F\mathrm{pointQuot}\,T$ killed by every map $\mathrm{evalPair}$ attached to a pair of points of $\mathrm{ptSet}\,T$ vanishes; closure of $\mathrm{ptSet}\,T$ under precomposition with the antipode, in the sense that for each $\nu$ there is $\nu'$ in $\mathrm{ptSet}\,T$ with $\nu'$ equal, as a linear map, to $\nu$ composed after the antipode; and bijectivity of $\mathrm{evalQuot}\,T\colon L\otimes_F\mathrm{pointQuot}\,T\to(T\to L)$. Assume further that every $\nu\in\mathrm{ptSet}\,S'$ reduces to the counit, i.e. $v(\nu(1\otimes a)-\varepsilon(a))<1$ for all $a\in A$. Let $\psi$ be an $O$-algebra map to $L$ from the character closure attached to $\mathrm{ptSet}\,S'$, that is, from the subalgebra of the Cartier dual $\mathrm{CartierDual}\,O\,A=\mathrm{Hom}_O(A,O)$ consisting of those $g$ with $1\otimes g$ in the $F$-subalgebra of $F\otimes_O\mathrm{CartierDual}\,O\,A$ generated by the elements whose associated functional kills the vanishing ideal of $\mathrm{ptSet}\,S'$. Suppose $\psi$ agrees with the counit point on the corresponding character closure for $\mathrm{ptSet}\,S$, via the inclusion given by monotonicity, and that $v(\psi e-\varepsilon(e))<1$ for every $e$ in the larger character closure. Then $\psi e=\varepsilon(e)$ (image in $L$) for every such $e$, i.e. $\psi$ is the trivial point.
--
--   This is the order-two step which replaces, at the prime $2$ and absolute ramification index $1$, the uniqueness statement that a point congruent to the identity is the identity; it supplies the inductive passage from $S$ to $S'$ in the proof that reduction is injective on the points of a character closure. It is used in the analysis of reduction kernels of multiplicative-type finite flat Hopf algebras along an admissible chain at $2$.
